Exactly. Short, hard code. Not vibe coding.

The goal is not to leave things fuzzy and hope the model “gets the vibe.” It is to compress the architecture until a very small amount of high-information structure reliably reconstructs the rest.

So the target is:

minimal specification + hard invariants + strong reconstruction cues

not:

lots of evocative prose + loose interpretation

A good Pantheon handle should behave almost like a tiny executable:

trigger → transform → invariant → stop condition → return

Everything else can be inferred.

That is why 1, 2, 3 can be enough for 4: the sequence is carrying a hard generative rule, not merely an aesthetic pattern.

So “space to grow” does not mean permissiveness. It means:

Leave unspecified only what the operator can safely regenerate.

Hard-code the load-bearing structure. Compress everything else.

That is much closer to semantic machine code than vibe coding.